WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.053
- [Instructor] In this video,

00:00:01.053 --> 00:00:02.960
I'm going to introduce you to a new type

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.170
of mathematical notation

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:07.380
that will seem fancy at first,

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:09.580
but hopefully you'll
appreciate is pretty useful

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:12.000
and also pretty straightforward.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.090
So let's just start with some
things that we already know.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.130
So I could have just a 10.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:20.320
I could take two 10s and
multiply them together,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.380
so 10 times 10, which
you know is equal to 100.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:27.050
I could take three 10s and
multiply them together.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:31.740
10 times 10 times 10 is equal to 1000.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.580
And I could do this
with any number of 10s.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:39.340
But at some point, if I'm
doing this with enough 10s,

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.990
it gets pretty hard to write.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:45.660
So for example, let's say I
were to do this with 10 10s.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.510
So if I were to go 10 times 10

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:53.510
times 10 times 10,
that's four, that's five,

00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:57.590
that's six, that's seven, that's eight,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:00:59.910
that's nine, that is 10 10s.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.880
Let's see, one, two, three, four,

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:04.630
five, six, seven, eight, nine, 10.

00:01:04.630 --> 00:01:06.030
This is going to be equal to,

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:07.930
even the number that it's equal to

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:10.170
is going to be quite hard to write.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:12.700
It's going to be one
followed by 10 zeroes.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.020
One, two, three, four, five,

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:17.710
six, seven, eight, nine, 10.

00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:18.720
We put the commas there

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:20.880
so it's just a little bit easier to read.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:23.470
This right over here is 10 billion,

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.630
and it's already getting
kinda hard to write,

00:01:25.630 --> 00:01:27.970
and imagine if we have 30 10s

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:30.020
that we were multiplying together.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.190
So mathematicians have
come up with a notation

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.930
and some ideas to be able
to write things like this

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.770
a little bit more elegantly.

00:01:39.770 --> 00:01:41.390
So the way they do this

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.780
is through something known as exponents.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:47.253
Exponents.

00:01:48.230 --> 00:01:50.970
And so 10 times 10,

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.070
we can rewrite as being equal to,

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.830
if I have two 10s and I'm
multiplying them together,

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.000
I could write this as 10

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.950
to the second power.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.080
That's how someone would say it.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.450
They would say 10 to the second power.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:06.940
That looks fancy,

00:02:06.940 --> 00:02:09.800
but all that means is let's take two 10s

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.800
and multiply them together

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.500
and we're going to get 100.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.730
Just so you're familiar with
some of the parts of this,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.920
the two would be called the exponent

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.090
and the 10 would be the base.

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:25.640
So 10 to the second power is 10 times 10

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.380
is equal to 100.

00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:31.840
So how would you write 10
times 10 times 10 or 1000?

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.700
How would you write that using exponents?

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.770
Pause this video and see
if you can figure that out.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.140
Well, as exactly as you
might have imagined,

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.400
we're taking a certain number of 10s

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:42.970
and we see we're taking three 10s

00:02:42.970 --> 00:02:45.110
and we're multiplying them together.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:48.020
So this would be 10 to the third power.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.440
10 is the base, three is the exponent.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.420
We would read this as
10 to the third power.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:54.900
If you ever saw 10 to the third power,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:58.110
that means hey, let me
multiply 10 times 10 times 10.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.540
That's the same thing as 1000.

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:02.840
So this is really another
way of writing 1000.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.230
And what about this
number here, 10 billion?

00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.700
What's a way that we could
write it using exponents?

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.500
Pause the video and figure that out.

00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:11.953
Well as you might have imagined,

00:03:11.953 --> 00:03:14.800
we were taking 10 10s and
multiplying them together.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:19.430
This is 10 to the 10th power.

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:20.960
And we can go the other way around.

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:23.170
If someone were to walk
up to you on the street

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:28.170
and say what is 10 to the fifth power?

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:29.443
What is that?

00:03:29.443 --> 00:03:31.250
What number that you're probably familiar

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:32.523
with would this be?

00:03:33.410 --> 00:03:36.020
Well this would mean that
we're going to take five 10s

00:03:36.020 --> 00:03:37.170
and multiply them together.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:42.170
So 10 times 10 times 10 times 10 times 10.

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.249
And so 10 times 10 is 100,

00:03:45.249 --> 00:03:46.960
100 times 10 is 1000,

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:49.550
1000 times 10 is 10000,

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:53.307
10000 times 10 is equal to 100000.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:56.840
So there you have it.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.920
You have the basics of exponents

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:00.980
when we're dealing with 10,

00:04:00.980 --> 00:04:02.410
and I know what you were thinking.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:05.630
Can I put another number
here instead of 10?

00:04:05.630 --> 00:04:07.840
And the simple answer is, you can,

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.633
but we're not gonna cover
that just yet in this video.

